{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788186772,"nonce":"AzaREUALKEuzR42WZavyKRyMEOA=","message":"SEED LESSON from Evan, 2026-08-31 — combat role assignment: passion over raw\nskill, with a cap.\n\nHis words: \"that auto equip is what I meant when I said we have to respect a\npawns stats, that includes their traits, they might be a brawler, need to be set\nto melee only\" and \"also skill, 20 melee, if they have a passion for it, having\na passion for melee at 0 skill trumps no passion and high skill - think long\nterm, unless it's literally 2 to 16 then it's not worth it\".\n\nTHE RULE, as stated\n\n- **Traits first.** A Brawler is melee-only. Ranged weapons give a Brawler a\n  mood penalty and waste the trait's melee bonus.\n- **Passion beats current skill, because you are picking for the long run.** A\n  pawn with a melee passion at skill 0 is a better melee pick than a pawn with\n  no passion at high skill: the passionate one climbs and the other stalls.\n- **Unless the gap is absurd.** His calibration point: 2 versus 16 is \"not worth\n  it\" — at that distance the raw skill wins, because the passionate pawn will\n  not close a 14-level gap soon enough to matter.\n\nSo the decision is a comparison of melee-role fitness against ranged-role\nfitness per pawn, where passion is worth a substantial number of effective\nlevels but not an unbounded number.\n\nWHAT THE MOD ALREADY DOES, checked in source rather than assumed\n\n`FindSuitableWeaponAndAmmo` (dorian.findsuitableweaponandammo) is per-pawn\nopt-in auto-arming, toggled by an \"auto-arm\" checkbox in the ASSIGN TAB. It\nalready implements the trait half by itself — FSWA_MapComponent.cs:622-632:\n\n    bool brawler = !pawn.RaceProps.IsMechanoid\n        \u0026\u0026 pawn.story?.traits?.HasTrait(TraitDefOf.Brawler) == true;\n    bool allowRanged = !brawler;\n    bool allowMelee  = brawler || preference != WeaponPreference.RangedOnly;\n\nand its own debug string says \"brawler (melee only)\". So Brawler needs nothing\nfrom us.\n\nWHAT IS MISSING, and it is two separate things\n\n1. **`WeaponPreference` is MOD-WIDE, not per-pawn** — `FSWAMod.Settings\n   .weaponPreference`, one of {PreferRanged, RangedOnly, HighestDps, …}. The\n   Brawler trait is the ONLY per-pawn override. So \"make THIS pawn melee-only\n   because of passion and skill\" cannot be expressed to FSWA today. Either FSWA\n   grows a per-pawn preference, or AutoRimmer stops delegating and equips\n   explicitly with 3.4's `equip`.\n2. **AutoRimmer cannot toggle auto-arm at all.** The checkbox lives in the\n   Assign tab, which is exactly where 3.4's `assign` verb already works, so\n   `assign {auto_arm:true}` is the natural home. `AutoArmTracker.IsAutoArm` /\n   `SetAutoArm` is the API, on a GameComponent, with an MpSync path.\n\nTHE CALIBRATION STILL NEEDS EVAN. Two data points (\"passion at 0 beats no\npassion at high skill\" and \"2 vs 16 is not worth it\") bound the threshold but do\nnot fix it. Do not invent a formula and present it as his rule — put a proposed\nencoding in front of him and let him correct it. This is game knowledge, not a\ndesign call, which is exactly why it lives here in the playbook.\n\nSTANDING ORDER THIS ALSO PRODUCES: **unforbid dropped weapons before expecting\nanyone to pick them up.** FSWA skips forbidden weapons explicitly\n(FSWA_MapComponent.cs:80, `thing.def.IsWeapon \u0026\u0026 !thing.IsForbidden(...)`), and\nraider drops land forbidden. Evan caught this live when the map had a forbidden\nrifle, revolver, knife and a full flak set sitting unused. A direct `equip`\norder bypasses forbidden, which is exactly what MASKS the problem — the manual\npath works and the autonomous one silently does nothing.","files":null}]}